<h2 id="arguments">The arguments for and against the triple lock</h2>

<p>These are the arguments for keeping the triple lock and for changing it.</p>

<div class="option-grid">
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="args-keep">For keeping the triple lock</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Most pensioners depend on it.</strong> {{ ("ppi-reliance" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ppi-reliance") }} {{ ("ageuk-largest-income" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ageuk-largest-income") }}</li>
      <li><strong>Pensioner poverty has not gone away.</strong> In the {{ ("low-income-ahc" | figure).period_label | lower }}, an estimated {{ m.f("low-income-ahc") }} of pensioners were in relative low income after housing costs, within a range of {{ ("low-income-ahc" | figure).range }}. {{ m.ref(("low-income-ahc" | figure).source) }} {{ ("ageuk-women" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ageuk-women") }}</li>
      <li><strong>It helps today's workers most.</strong> {{ ("ppi-future-workers" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ppi-future-workers") }}</li>
      <li><strong>More pensioners, not the lock, cause most of the rise in State Pension spending.</strong> {{ ("ppi-demographics" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ppi-demographics") }} The Office for Budget Responsibility projects State Pension spending to rise by {{ m.f("sp-rise-gdp-total") }} between 2030-31 and 2075-76, and attributes {{ m.f("tl-share-of-rise") }} of it to the triple lock. {{ m.ref(("tl-share-of-rise" | figure).source) }}</li>
      <li><strong>The level is about 30% of typical pay.</strong> In {{ ("nsp-share-median-earnings" | figure).period_label }} the full new State Pension was {{ m.f("nsp-share-median-earnings") }} of median full-time earnings. {{ m.ref(("nsp-share-median-earnings" | figure).source) }} {{ ("pc-30-percent" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("pc-30-percent") }}</li>
      <li><strong>It supports private saving.</strong> {{ ("pc-firm-foundation" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("pc-firm-foundation") }}</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
  <article class="option">
    <h3 id="args-change">For changing it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It has cost more than expected.</strong> {{ m.f("tl-cost-vs-earnings-2029-30") }} a year by 2029-30 compared with earnings uprating, against {{ m.f("tl-cost-initial-assumption") }} on the first assumption. {{ m.ref(("tl-cost-vs-earnings-2029-30" | figure).source) }}</li>
      <li><strong>Its future cost can only be estimated, with wide uncertainty.</strong> Between {{ m.f("ifs-tl-cost-2050-low") }} and {{ m.f("ifs-tl-cost-2050-high") }} a year in 2050. {{ m.ref(("ifs-tl-cost-2050-high" | figure).source) }} Both ends are the extra cost of the triple lock over raising the pension with earnings alone, not the whole bill. How each rule compares is in <a href="#increases">the options above</a> and in <a href="/reform-options/compare/">the rule comparison</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>It rewards volatility, not need.</strong> {{ ("rf-volatility" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rf-volatility") }}</li>
      <li><strong>It has no stated target.</strong> {{ ("ifs-explicit-goal" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-explicit-goal") }}</li>
      <li><strong>Fairness between generations.</strong> {{ ("rf-living-standards" | claim).statement }} {{ ("rf-income-level" | claim).statement }} {{ ("rf-income-same-level" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rf-living-standards") }}</li>
      <li><strong>It is not aimed at poverty.</strong> {{ ("rf-poverty-since-2011" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rf-poverty-since-2011") }} On the same measure, after housing costs, the latest official level is the {{ "low-income-ahc" | fig }} given in the arguments for keeping it. The triple lock applies the same percentage to the full rates, whatever the income of the person who receives them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</div>

<p>The Pensions Policy Institute and the Office for Budget Responsibility agree that more pensioners add more to State Pension spending than the triple lock does. They use different dates and baselines, so their figures cannot be compared directly. The Office for Budget Responsibility's projection is set out on the page on <a href="/reform-options/costs/#public-finances">what the State Pension costs</a>.</p>

<h2 id="evidence-and-judgement">What evidence can settle, and what it cannot</h2>

<h3 id="evidence-can-establish">Evidence can establish</h3>
<ul>
  <li>What each rule does to the rate under given conditions.</li>
  <li>Roughly what the bill is, and that estimates of cost and saving depend on the comparison chosen.</li>
  <li>That savings from a change start small and grow.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="evidence-cannot-establish">Evidence cannot establish</h3>
<ul>
  <li>What prices and earnings will do, and so what any rule will cost.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="judgements-about-fairness">Judgements about fairness</h3>
<ul>
  <li>What the State Pension should be worth compared with pay.</li>
  <li>How to weigh today's pensioners against today's workers, who are tomorrow's pensioners.</li>
  <li>How much certainty people are owed when they plan for retirement.</li>
</ul>

